    {"ip": "185.220.101.42", "inferred_role": "external_c2_keylogger_exfil_endpoint", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:48:29+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-26T02:55:14+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Execution (PowerShell loader)", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1566.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-11-26 02:35:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-EXEC-09 (10.30.7.18) logged 'powershell.exe -nop -enc <base64>' with parentImage=outlook.exe, user=CORP\\thierry.b. The parent=outlook.exe is the smoking-gun pivot: a PowerShell process spawned directly from the Outlook client is almost never a legitimate enterprise workflow — it is the canonical signature of a malicious-attachment / phishing-link clickthrough. The '-enc' flag plus '-nop' (NoProfile) is the standard offensive-tooling launch pattern (Empire / Cobalt Strike / Sliver default loaders). User is an EXECUTIVE (high-value target — finance / strategy / M&A material on the keyboard).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with image=powershell.exe + parentImage=outlook.exe + commandLine containing '-enc' is a categorical phishing-clickthrough indicator. User profile (executive) elevates business impact.", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:35:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Masquerading (Payload Drop)", "technique": "T1036.005 + T1105", "what_happened": "At 02:42:18 (7 minutes after the loader), Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) on WS-EXEC-09: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\thierry.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\winlog.exe, signed=false, hash=e9c8d2a1b3f7c6d5a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1. The name 'winlog.exe' is a deliberate masquerade — it impersonates a legitimate-sounding Windows binary (the real Windows Event Log service is 'wevtsvc.dll' hosted in svchost.exe; there is no Microsoft-shipped 'winlog.exe'). The location AppData\\Roaming is a writable-by-user path that does NOT require administrator privileges, the standard low-privilege persistence drop location. signed=false confirms the binary is not Authenticode-signed (no MS cert, no enterprise cert).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 with targetFilename ending in .exe + signed=false + path matching %AppData%\\Roaming\\* is the canonical malicious-payload-drop pattern. The 7-minute gap from the loader is consistent with PowerShell loader → download stager → decrypt → write payload.", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:42:18+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution (Payload Launch)", "technique": "T1059 + T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 02:42:42 (24 seconds after the drop), Sysmon Event 1: image=C:\\Users\\thierry.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\winlog.exe, parentImage=powershell.exe, user=CORP\\thierry.b. The freshly-dropped masquerading binary launched immediately from the PowerShell loader = classic stager → payload handoff. Now the unsigned binary is the LIVE process; the original powershell.exe loader has done its job and likely exits or persists in a worker thread.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image equals the targetFilename from the preceding Event 11, parentImage=powershell.exe, same user. The 24-second gap is consistent with automated handoff (no human latency).", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:42:42+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Collection (Input Capture — SMOKING GUN)", "technique": "T1056.001", "what_happened": "At 02:48:13 (5 minutes 31 seconds after winlog.exe launch), Sysmon Event 10 (ProcessAccess) fired on WS-EXEC-09: SourceImage=C:\\Users\\thierry.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\winlog.exe, TargetImage=C:\\Windows\\explorer.exe, GrantedAccess=0x1F0FFF (PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS), CallTrace contains 'SetWindowsHookExW'. This is THE DEFINITIVE keylogger fingerprint: SetWindowsHookExW with a WH_KEYBOARD_LL hook type installs a SYSTEM-WIDE low-level keyboard hook — the hooked process (explorer.exe, present in every desktop session) receives EVERY KEYSTROKE typed anywhere on the system before the keystroke reaches its intended target window. Simultaneously at 02:48:13, the EDR memory-monitoring sensor fired INPUT_HOOK ALERT: process=winlog.exe, target=explorer.exe = system-wide hook installation. This is a confirmed keylogger; the question is no longer 'is this a keylogger' but 'how much has it already captured'.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 10 with CallTrace containing 'SetWindowsHookExW' from a non-Microsoft-signed SourceImage targeting explorer.exe is a categorical T1056.001 indicator. EDR INPUT_HOOK ALERT is the same event from a different sensor — two independent attestations of the same installation.", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:48:13+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Collection (Keystroke Log File Writes)", "technique": "T1056.001", "what_happened": "At 02:48:24 (11 seconds after the hook installation), Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\thierry.b\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\klog.dat. The keylogger is now WRITING captured keystrokes to a local staging file. The .dat extension + Temp location is the canonical 'staging file before exfiltration' pattern. Every minute the keylogger runs, klog.dat grows with whatever the executive types: passwords, emails, board documents, M&A deal terms, account credentials for SaaS apps, internal chat content.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 with targetFilename in %Temp% appearing within seconds of an Event 10 / SetWindowsHookExW installation is the staging-file confirmation. klog.dat naming is a tool default.", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:48:24+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command & Control + Exfiltration", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1041", "what_happened": "At 02:48:29 (5 seconds after the first klog.dat write), Sysmon Event 3: winlog.exe initiated outbound DNS for keysync.cloud which resolved to 185.220.101.42, followed by an HTTPS POST of 12 KB to that IP:443. At 02:55:14 (6 minutes 45 seconds later), a SECOND HTTPS POST of 14 KB to the same destination. The 6-minute-45-second cadence indicates a PERIODIC exfiltration timer (likely scheduled to fire every 5-10 minutes, with jitter). Each POST contains the contents of klog.dat (or a sliding-window-since-last-exfil chunk). The domain 'keysync.cloud' is a TYPOSQUAT of legitimate-sounding cloud-sync services and was registered for this campaign; the destination 185.220.101.42 is a known C&C+Spyware node hosted in NL via Hetzner.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=winlog.exe + destination IP/domain external. The first POST 5s after first klog.dat write = exfil-cadence indicator. Second POST 6m45s later confirms periodic timer (not single-shot).", "first_seen": "2027-11-26T02:48:29+0100"}
  ],
